    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2999-dd, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7879\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to requiring parity\nin the standards of dental telehealth services\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill requires dental telehealth services to adhere to the standards\nof appropriate patient care required in other dental health care\nsettings and prohibits dental telehealth providers from attempting to\nwaive liability for its telehealth services.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill amends Public Health Law S2999dd.\n\nSection 1 requires dental telehealth services to adhere to the standards\nof appropriate care required in other dental health care settings,\nrequires dental telehealth providers to identify themselves to patients\nand provide their New York state license number, prohibits a dental\ntelehealth provider from attempting to waive liability in advance of\nproviding services and prohibits a dental telehealth provider from\nattempting to prevent a patient from filing a complaint with any govern-\nmental agency or authority.\n\nSection 2 provides that the bill shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn dentistry, particularly in orthodontics, there are unqualified tele-\nhealth providers taking advantage of the public and causing harm to\npatients by failing to adhere to recognized standards of care.  Unli-\ncensed people are performing procedures that require a professional\nlicense and professional skill to perform. Patients are not undergoing\nproper examinations, necessary x-rays, or reviews of medical and dental\nhistory before orthodontic treatments are done.  Such behaviors by\nlicensed dentists would lead to malpractice and/or professional disci-\npline charges. To evade such possibilities, dental telehealth providers\ncoerce patients to agree not to file any complaints with any government\nauthorities and to agree to waive any liability of the telehealth\nprovider for bad or improper care. The law must be amended to make clear\nthat such practices will not be tolerated.\n\nThe failure to address these basic patient safeguards could cause\npatients to lose trust in telehealth and to consider it an inferior\nhealth care delivery model.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
